+# Tool Input Validation
+
+| Field  | Value                |
+| ------ | -------------------- |
+| Issue  | re-cinq/HALEngine#37 |
+| Status | Shipped              |
+
+A tool's `inputSchema` is not decoration: it is the contract the model's tool call is held to, and `ToolRegistry.execute` is the one place that can hold every tool to it. Validation enforces `required`, declared types and `enum` only — it does not inject `additionalProperties: false`, coerce types, or apply defaults, and there is no opt-out flag. A call the schema rejects does not throw: it comes back as a `ToolResponse` the model can read and correct within the existing tool-round budget, because a throw today escapes the orchestrator's tool-call `Promise.all`, fires `onError` and reaches the WebSocket handler as a generic transport error that never sends the stream-end frame.
+
+## What the registry enforces
+
+- A call whose input lacks a property the schema lists in `required` is rejected with a `ToolResponse` whose `result` names the missing property and its constraint, and the tool's executor is never invoked ([validated by: rejects input missing a required property with a ToolResponse naming it, and never calls the executor](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L25)).
+- Input the schema accepts reaches the executor exactly as the model sent it — unlisted keys are kept, no default is injected, no type is coerced — and the executor runs exactly once ([validated by: passes schema-valid input through to the executor unchanged, extra keys included, exactly once](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L42)).
+- A rejection names the failing property path and the constraint that failed, and names no property value ([validated by: rejects a wrong-typed value naming the property path and expected type without the offending value](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L58)).
+- A schema that cannot compile fails at `register` time with an error naming the tool, before any conversation uses it ([validated by: throws at registration time for a schema that does not compile, naming the tool](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L76)).
+- A schema produced by a function-valued `ToolDefinitionSource` is resolved and compiled per call, cached by the resolved schema object, so each call is validated against the schema current at that call rather than a stale one ([validated by: validates a function-valued definition against the schema current at each call, not a stale one](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L97)).
+- `execute` does not re-compile a static tool's schema: ten calls against one registration compile it once ([validated by: compiles a static tool schema once across ten execute calls](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L127)).
+- `execute` throws `Unknown tool: <name>` for a name that is not registered — the one throw the registry keeps ([validated by: throws Unknown tool, naming the tool, for an unregistered name](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L139)).
+- A rejected call carries no input value out of the engine: no log line it produces and no returned text names the input's values ([validated by: keeps an identifying input value out of the rejection text and every log line](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L146)).
+
+## The tool loop sees the rejection
+
+- A rejected tool call reaches the model as an ordinary tool result rather than an exception: `processMessage` resolves, the tool loop continues inside its existing round budget, and the provider sees the rejection text on the next round ([validated by: returns a rejected tool call to the model as a tool result and lets a corrected retry complete](../../src/orchestration/chatOrchestrator.inputValidation.acceptance.test.ts#L46)).
+
+## Out of scope
+
+Changing `Unknown tool: <name>` from a throw to a returned result — the same failure class handled the opposite way, tracked as its own issue, since it affects direct callers. Validating tool *output*; hallucination detection and an evaluation harness; per-tool authorization — a schema-valid call for someone else's data is still valid, and authorization is a separate `ToolContext` boundary concern no issue in this epic owns; retry, timeout and rate limiting around tool execution; and any actual tool implementation, which is written against this contract later. An executor that used to hand-validate its input now receives only input the schema already accepts — that migration note belongs to the pull request and `CHANGELOG.md`, not to this spec.
